From f96b007c3303c337fd027a6ef70b50cbcfe5f0c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Carlos Bravo <cbravoa@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 21:27:15 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] memory_pressure: add a Linux system memory pressure evaluator

//content has no memory pressure source on Linux:
CreateDefaultSystemEvaluator() returned nullptr outside Windows, macOS and
Fuchsia, so a browser under a cgroup limit allocated until the OOM killer
ended it, though a pressure signal frees 15-40% on real sites.

The evaluator polls (250 ms) every cgroup v2 budget from the process's
cgroup up to the root (memory.max / memory.high against memory.current
minus inactive page cache) plus the system (MemAvailable/MemTotal), and
votes moderate below 30% left and critical below 15% of the tightest one.
PSI "some avg10" >= 20 raises it to at least moderate. Thresholds and
period are params of the LinuxMemoryPressureEvaluator feature (enabled by
default). Kernel files are read with ReadFileToStringNonBlocking on the
evaluator's sequence. Level changes are logged at
--vmodule=system_memory_pressure_evaluator_linux=1.

Under a 256 MB cap it kept 8 of 10 test sites working, against 6 without
it; under 192 MB, 5 against 3. Page load times were unchanged.
